// Code the program did not write.
//
// Two facts say a macro wrote a function, and they make two claims
// (Argus.Extractors.Generated):
//
// - macro_written(f): every clause of f was written by another module's
//   macro (an Elixir `use`), or marked `generated: true`, or defined by
//   an OTP header or a parser generator (Erlang: yecc's runtime from
//   yeccpre.hrl, an installed application's include): the module wrote
//   none of it. The word for "the program did not write this function".
// - macro_generated(f, by): the definition's metadata names `by`'s
//   macro. The metadata is the FIRST clause's, so a function whose first
//   clause a `use` injected ahead of the module's own clauses is named
//   too (Sequin's ProcessMetrics puts a `:metrics` clause ahead of the
//   module's handle_info/2 clauses). Read it for `by`, never alone as
//   "generated": a rule that did judged the module's own clauses as the
//   macro's.
//
// library_written(f): every clause of f a module outside the program
// wrote (`use Cachex.Warmer`'s handle_info/2, a generated parser's yecc
// runtime). Its call sites and handlers are the library's protocol, and
// a finding on it points at a `use` line or a generated parser, where
// the program has nothing to change. What the program's own macro writes
// is the program's — the fix belongs in that macro — and a library
// analyzed with the program is the program.
//
// Included by imports.dl; an analysis that reads it runs the Generated
// extractor.
.decl library_written(func: symbol)

library_written(f) :-
  macro_written(f),
  macro_generated(f, by),
  !program_module(by).

// A module the analyzed beams define.
.decl program_module(mod: symbol)

program_module(m) :- function_def(_, m, _, _, _).
